From 9b0a1d614aafb967859f1cb62f21dfa27cf9a9e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oncology Review <review@example.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 07:44:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Oncology module: fix patient-safety, data-integrity and
 access-control gaps

Review of the oncology module (chemotherapy engine, chart/encounter, notes,
bookings, legacy chemo prescriptions). Every defect below was reproduced
before fixing; most on real PostgreSQL 16, which is the production target
(the module only "worked" on SQLite through type affinity).

CHEMOTHERAPY ENGINE / API  (departments/medicine/chemotherapy_engine.py)
- No invented patient data: calculate_bsa() returned 1.73 m2 for any invalid
  input and the API defaulted missing/garbage height/weight to 170 cm / 70 kg.
  nan/inf were accepted (HTTP 200, BSA nan/inf). Now rejected with a clear
  error; plausibility bounds 30-250 cm / 1-350 kg; formula whitelisted.
- Fail closed: the cumulative-dose lookup did `except Exception: pass` and
  returned {}, silently disabling every lifetime-cap check (and the table was
  never migrated, see below). Now raises -> API returns 503.
- Cancelled orders no longer count toward lifetime totals.
- Lifetime mg/m2 is accumulated per order with that order's own BSA instead of
  dividing a mg total by today's BSA (which under-detects the cap when BSA grows).
- Units: absolute doses/cumulatives were labelled "mg/m2" (e.g. "109.2 mg/m2")
  in the API and UI; bleomycin cap message said "units/m2".
- AC-T gave doxorubicin+cyclophosphamide+paclitaxel every cycle. It is
  sequential (AC x4 then T x4): drugs are now phase-gated by cycle.
- Vincristine single-dose cap now also comes from the global cap table so a
  future protocol cannot bypass it.
- Save endpoint: requires an existing patient; validates cycle/total cycles;
  rejects duplicate active cycle orders (also a partial unique index);
  a lifetime-cap warning is a hard stop unless a documented justification
  (>=10 chars) is supplied and stored; garbage numbers are 400, not 500;
  order creation is audited and rolled back if the audit row cannot be written.
- New: order status workflow (ORDERED->PREPARED->ADMINISTERED, cancel with
  reason, role-gated, audited) and a per-patient order list. Without a cancel
  path an erroneous order permanently inflated cumulative dose.
- Builder UI: no pre-filled 170/70, patient name shown, server errors shown
  instead of leaving stale doses on screen, correct unit labels, cycle sent to
  the calculator, override prompt flow, HTML-escaped output, |tojson id.

SCHEMA  (models + migration e5f6a7b8c9d0)
- chemotherapy_regimen_orders had NO migration and the model was not imported
  by departments.models: databases built with `flask db upgrade` had no chemo
  order table. Model registered; migration creates the table (or adds the new
  columns + patient FK NOT VALID if it exists from create_all()).
- oncology_notes had no void columns: the "void" route set unmapped attributes,
  flashed success and persisted NOTHING (existing test used
  getattr(note, "is_voided", True) and passed vacuously). Columns added.
- onco_patients.stage widened 20->100 (CancerStage.label is 100).
- Migration refuses (with an actionable message) to add the unique index over
  pre-existing duplicate active orders rather than altering clinical data.
  Downgrade never drops the chemo table. Verified on PostgreSQL 16: upgrade of
  (a) a database lacking the table, as the migration chain produces, and (b) a
  create_all()-era database with the old table shape; duplicate-order refusal
  and recovery; downgrade/upgrade round trip; zero Alembic drift vs. the models.
  NOTE: the repo's full chain cannot be run from an empty PostgreSQL database
  for an unrelated pre-existing reason (an earlier revision uses
  `BOOLEAN DEFAULT 0`), so (a) was built with the original code minus the table
  and stamped at the previous head c3d4e5f6a7b8.

PATIENT IDENTITY
- OncoPatient.patient_id is a string FK to patients.patient_id but the
  encounter view queried/wrote the integer PK and the enrol form posted it:
  encounter page 500'd on PostgreSQL (varchar = integer) and enrolment
  violated the FK.
- Legacy prescriptions: onco_prescriptions.onco_patient_id is an INTEGER FK to
  onco_patients.id but routes stored/compared the patient number (DataError /
  "integer = varchar" 500 on PostgreSQL). Now resolved via the enrolment row.
- Fuzzy ILIKE patient lookups replaced by exact matches. In the AI summary the
  consent check was exact but the patient was resolved with ILIKE '%x%' on id
  OR name, so consent for "P1" released the notes of "P10" to the LLM
  (reproduced). Bookings validated fuzzily then stored the raw text.

CHART / NOTES
- Editing oncology details silently discarded edits (form re-populated from DB
  before POST was read). Names (not ids) are stored, stage is checked against
  the cancer type, PRG redirect after POST (refresh no longer duplicates notes).
- Voided notes are flagged in the UI, cannot be edited, and are excluded from
  the AI summary; the void form now actually sends the required reason.
- Note amendments keep the previous text in the append-only audit log
  (SECURITY.md: immutable clinical history).
- AI summary: disclosure audited before the model call, prompt size bounded.
- Stage filter fetched /get_stages, which 404s (blueprint is mounted at
  /medicine), so the dependent stage dropdown never filtered.

ACCESS CONTROL
- Oncology chart, enrolment, note edit, bookings, legacy prescriptions and
  disease admin were login_required only: a lab_tech could read and write the
  chart. Now role-gated (write: doctor/medicine/oncology/admin; read adds
  nurse/pharmacist; scheduling adds nurse). /get_stages was unauthenticated.
- Legacy prescription: prescriber came from a free-text form field (spoofable);
  now the logged-in user. Drugs must belong to the regimen, >=1 drug, regimen
  must be Active, patient must be enrolled.
- Removed print() calls that wrote patient ids/notes to stdout.
- rbac.has_any_role(): non-aborting twin of roles_required (same semantics).

TESTS
- tests/test_oncology_hardening.py: 171 regression tests. Run against the
  original code 138 fail; the other 33 pin behaviour that was already correct
  (e.g. anonymous users bounced) or that the old code satisfied incidentally.
- Four existing tests encoded the old behaviour and were updated: calculate_bsa
  (0,0)==1.73; two engine tests ran with no app/DB at all and passed only
  because the history lookup swallowed the error; builder test used a
  non-existent patient. The vacuous void assertion now checks persisted state.

BEHAVIOUR CHANGES TO BE AWARE OF
- Saving an order over a lifetime cap now needs a documented justification.
- The calculate/save APIs no longer accept missing biometrics (no defaults).
- Non-clinical roles lose access to oncology patient data.
- The legacy prescription "Prescribed by" field is ignored (login is used).

NOT CHANGED - needs clinical / product sign-off (see review notes)
- Protocol library values (incl. AC-T phase ranges and default cycle counts)
  and bleomycin cap (300 vs. commonly cited 400 units).
- ABVD is given on days 1 and 15: one order/cycle under-counts cumulative dose.
- No cross-anthracycline (epirubicin/daunorubicin) equivalence; no ANC/platelet,
  renal or hepatic gating; no independent double-check for chemo preparation.
- The encounter page only renders forms/notes for patients with a booking.
- Legacy prescription doses remain free text (the chemo builder is the safe path).
